'en_content'=><<<'HTML'
<p>Small white dots on a ham can be alarming, but not every white mark is the same. A dry-cured leg may show <strong>tyrosine crystals, surface mould or mites</strong>. So-called <strong>ham mites</strong> belong to the last group and, unlike crystals, they can move.</p>
<p>One mite associated with cured foods is <em>Tyrophagus putrescentiae</em>, a tiny organism that can proliferate on foods rich in protein and fat when environmental conditions suit it. Its presence is a storage and quality problem that is best addressed early.</p>
<h2>How to recognise ham mites</h2>
<p>The mites are extremely small and individual animals can be difficult to see. Useful signs include <strong>tiny pale specks that slowly move</strong>, very fine dust collecting in cracks, or residue that seems to return after the surface has been cleaned.</p>
<p>Inspect the rind, shank area, cracks, hanging cord, ham stand and the edge of the exposed cut under good light. With a more obvious infestation, movement may become visible if you watch the surface carefully for several seconds.</p>
<h2>Do not confuse them with tyrosine crystals</h2>
<p>Tyrosine crystals appear inside the muscle as hard little white points. They result from protein changes during maturation and <strong>do not move</strong>. A white point embedded in a slice and remaining completely fixed does not behave like a mite.</p>
<p>Our guide to <a href="/en/white-crystals-on-iberian-ham-what-they-are-tyrosine-safe-to-eat/">white tyrosine crystals in cured ham</a> explains the phenomenon. Location is an important clue: tyrosine is usually seen within the meat; mites mainly develop on surfaces and in crevices.</p>
<h2>They are not the same as mould either</h2>
<p>Surface mould can develop on the rind during curing. It forms patches, films or coatings that do not independently crawl. Mites are tiny animals and a population can create the appearance of moving dust.</p>
<p>If you are unsure whether you are seeing normal surface mould or a deeper problem, see our guide to <a href="/en/mould-on-iberian-ham-when-normal-when-to-worry-what-to-do/">mould on Iberian ham</a>.</p>
<h2>Why do mites appear on ham?</h2>
<p>Mites do better when <strong>relatively high temperature, elevated humidity, available food and limited ventilation</strong> come together. They are a known challenge in dry-curing environments, but they can also appear during prolonged storage at home.</p>
<p>A leg that remains open for a long time, a warm humid room, food debris around the stand or fatty material trapped in cracks can help them persist. Finding mites does not by itself tell you how the entire ham was produced; conditions after purchase also matter.</p>
<h2>What to do if you see a few specks on the surface</h2>
<p>First, <strong>stop carving and check whether there is actual movement</strong>. Clean the surroundings and ham stand so no food debris remains. If the issue appears limited to the outer surface, photograph it and contact the producer or seller before extensively trimming the ham, particularly when the purchase is recent.</p>
<p>Traditional handling can include cleaning the rind and external fat when the problem is very superficial, but a household clean-up should not be treated as a guarantee of safety. If the infestation is broad or you are unsure how far it extends, the producer is better placed to assess the leg.</p>
<h2>When surface cleaning is not enough</h2>
<p>Be more cautious if mites repeatedly appear on the <strong>edible cut, deep cavities or internal areas</strong>; if there are large numbers; if unusual odour, wetness or texture appears as well; or if the problem returns immediately after cleaning.</p>
<p>In those circumstances, do not keep eating slices while trying to cut past the issue. Isolate the ham, prevent contact with other nearby dry or cured foods and contact the seller.</p>
<h2>Do mites mean the ham is poor quality?</h2>
<p>Not necessarily. They can occur on well-made cured products when environmental conditions favour them. That does not mean an active infestation should be treated as normal or ignored. A known curing risk is different from accepting a visibly affected ham without assessing its extent.</p>
<h2>How to reduce the risk at home</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep the ham in a cool, dry, ventilated place away from heat.</li>
<li>Avoid very humid enclosed spaces.</li>
<li>Keep the stand clean and remove fallen scraps.</li>
<li>Do not wrap the leg for long periods in material that traps moisture.</li>
<li>Inspect rind, cracks and the area around the cut occasionally.</li>
<li>If a whole ham takes many weeks to finish, consider whether sliced packs or a smaller piece better suits your household.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Clean the storage area too</h2>
<p>If mites are confirmed, do not focus only on the ham. Clean the stand, tray, table and any surface that may hold food particles. Check other cured foods or cheeses stored very close by so a wider problem is not missed.</p>
<h2>What if the ham also smells wrong?</h2>
<p>Mites should not be used to explain every abnormal aroma. A chemical, putrid or strongly unpleasant smell needs separate assessment. See our article on <a href="/en/ham-smells-like-ammonia-why-it-happens-when-it-is-a-problem/">ammonia-like smells in cured ham</a> for another diagnostic guide.</p>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p><strong>Ham mites are real mites, not crystals or simple stains.</strong> They are recognised mainly by their surface location and the movement of tiny pale specks, and they thrive more easily in warm humid conditions. A very superficial finding should be checked and controlled; extensive, internal or otherwise abnormal infestation justifies stopping consumption and asking the producer or seller to assess the product.</p>
